<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The metrics investors and lenders use</h2>
    <p>The <a href="../../calculators/commercial-real-estate.html">commercial real estate calculator</a> builds an Annual Property Operating Data (APOD) statement and reports the measures below. Each answers a different question, so a deal can look good on one and weak on another.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Net operating income (NOI)</td><td>Effective gross income − operating expenses</td><td>What the property earns before financing and taxes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cap rate</td><td>NOI ÷ price</td><td>The unleveraged yield; how the price compares with income</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cash-on-cash return</td><td>Pre-tax cash flow ÷ cash invested</td><td>What your own money earns in year 1, after the loan</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DSCR</td><td>NOI ÷ annual debt service</td><td>How comfortably income covers the mortgage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Debt yield</td><td>NOI ÷ loan amount</td><td>Lender's return if it had to take the property back</td></tr>
      <tr><td>IRR</td><td>Rate that sets all cash flows' present value to 0</td><td>Total return over the hold, including the sale</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Leverage cuts both ways</h2>
    <p>Borrowing improves returns only when the property earns more than the debt costs. Compare the cap rate with the loan constant, which is the annual payment as a percentage of the loan. A loan at 6.75% amortized over 25 years has a constant of about 8.3%. If the property's cap rate is below that, each borrowed dollar costs more than it earns, and the first-year cash-on-cash return falls below the cap rate. Many deals in higher-rate periods carry this "negative leverage" and rely on rent growth and the eventual sale to make the numbers work.</p>

    <h2>Questions that change the numbers most</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Are the rents real?</strong> Underwrite from the current rent roll and leases, not market rents in a marketing brochure. Look for concessions, free months, and tenants behind on payments.</li>
      <li><strong>What happens to property taxes after the sale?</strong> In many places a sale triggers reassessment closer to the purchase price, which can raise one of the largest expenses immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>When do leases roll?</strong> Several leases expiring in the same year can mean vacancy, leasing commissions, and tenant improvement costs at once.</li>
      <li><strong>What is the exit cap rate?</strong> A half-point change in the cap rate at sale can move the property's value by 6 to 8%. Assuming you'll sell at the same or a lower cap rate than you bought at is optimistic underwriting.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The calculator is a pre-tax planning model. Depreciation, tax treatment, and loan terms specific to your deal should be reviewed with a CPA, a lender, and the appropriate legal counsel before committing.</p>
  </div>
</section>
